FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] phases/phase-088/REPORT.md:115 - The gate row claims `cargo test --all-targets` = "1976 passed", and that number cannot be derived from anything in the phase - the base measured by phases/phase-087/REPORT.md:137 is "1766 passed", this phase's own "Tests added" table (REPORT.md:66-77) lists 9 new test functions (one row is a rewrite of an existing test, not a new one), and 1766 + 9 = 1775. This phase's own committed round-1 REPORT recorded "1766 → 1774 (the 8 new tests)" for the same gate on a tree that differs from this one by exactly one test; the two reports of the same gate on near-identical trees differ by 202. `--all-targets` also excludes doctests, so it cannot exceed the `cargo test` count (2 doctests, REPORT.md:116). A gate number that is the only evidence for a green gate and that does not reconcile with the phase's own arithmetic is the "fake completion" class, not a typo I can wave through - it must have been measured on a different tree or mis-transcribed (1976 vs 1775/1777). What would settle it: re-run `cargo test --all-targets` in this tree and paste the real count, or state the command and tree the 1976 came from. VERIFIED: traced the arithmetic from phases/phase-087/REPORT.md:137, phases/phase-088/REPORT.md:66-77, :115, and the committed round-1 REPORT in the change set; did not run the suite (read-only).
2. [MINOR] tests/loop_bounds_test.rs:718 - The assertion does not test the claim its own comment and the report make. The fixture ends with `say n` (loop_bounds_test.rs:705), and `say` returns `Value::Nothing` (src/interpreter.rs:1760-1765), so `assert_eq!(outcome, redblue::Value::Nothing)` pins the value of `say`, not "a loop statement is worth nothing" - the comment at :713-717 and REPORT.md:73 ("a loop statement is worth `nothing`") and REPORT.md:161 ("`repeat 4 times` to `Value::Nothing`") all claim the loop's value is what is asserted, and it is not. Fix: end the fixture with the loop (`set n to 0\nrepeat 4 times\n    set n to n + 1\nend`) and assert the value of that program, or drop the claim from the comment and the report. A reader who trusts the report will not re-check that the loop's value is pinned anywhere.
3. [MINOR] phases/phase-088/REPORT.md:89-98 - The "every one, with its reason" table cites stale line numbers for 6 of its 10 rows: it says `loop_bounds_test.rs:271` (the fn is at :281), `:308` (:326), `:322` (:346), `:586` (:642), `:596` (:657) and `:637` (:759); only `:159` and `text_split_join_test.rs:759` are right. The "Tests added" table above it (REPORT.md:66-77) was updated to the current lines, so the report is half-current. Fix: re-derive the ten rows' line numbers from the tree; a report whose index of its own changes sends the next reader to the wrong test.
4. [MINOR] phases/phase-088/REPORT.md:81-85 - The measurement the phase rests on does not add up inside the report: the prose says the scanner "found **10** tests", but the table it introduces (REPORT.md:87-98) names 9 tests individually plus a row "redblue_test.rs × 5" - 14 tests - and FINDINGS.md:46-47 repeats "the number is **10** ... all ten were fixed ... (the table is in `REPORT.md`)". The scan's own filter is also stated as "all 63 files in `tests/`" while the tree holds 68 `tests/*.rs` files. This is the same defect the phase set out to remove in the suite - a count that is asserted rather than derived - and the "twenty → ten" correction the next audit is asked to re-run rests on it. Fix: state one number and make the table's rows sum to it (or say "9 files, 14 tests"), and publish the scanner's file list so the next audit can re-run it.
5. [MINOR] phases/phase-088/FINDINGS.md:5 - The citations a follow-up phase is sent to are wrong: `run_source` is at src/lib.rs:94 (not :92), `result.map(|_| ())` is at src/lib.rs:110 (not :108), `run_source_value` is at src/lib.rs:120 (not :118), and `DELIVERY_SUBJECT_PREFIX` is at tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs:242 (not :235 as FINDINGS.md:106 says). REPORT.md:206-207 has the same reference right at :110, so the two files disagree. REPORT.md:122-123 also cites "the pipeline's `phases/phase-088/.base`" as the source of the base ref; there is no `.base` file under `phases/` in this tree, so that citation resolves to nothing for a reader. Fix: re-derive the four line numbers and either point at where the base ref really lives or say plainly that it is outside the checkout.
